Start();
include(unitTests);
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking UnitTests Utilities list;

/*****************************************************************************
    litFlagProbe -- ITEM 2 RECON, 2026-08-16. Does the isLiteral flag survive
    to the sites that read it?

    THE CLAIM UNDER TEST, Tony's direct observation as relayed by Clay: that
    isLiteral does not carry across rStuff duplication, nor across the handoff
    from TraiTdata into TraiT.

    The read sites that matter are in aCTionTraiTdata, which tests isRule
    together with the negation of isLiteral, and in aCTionDefinE, whose
    tag-swap arm tests isLiteral alone. Both read the flag off a node that may
    first have been duplicated, and that duplication runs only when the node
    already carries rStuff.

    isLiteraL is GroupField seventeen, so incant can read it directly. This
    prints it for a labelled literal, a bare literal, a reference term and a
    rule, so that a set value and a clear value both appear. A probe that
    printed only one of them could pass while measuring nothing.

    PREAMBLE IS LOAD-BEARING. The two includes must precede the search
    statement or search fails token by token, after which print emits zero
    bytes at exit zero. Measured 2026-08-16 on the first draft of litProbe.

    HEADER IS ALSO LOAD-BEARING, and this is bear-trap twenty-seven. An
    earlier draft of this header quoted the read-site test verbatim, operators
    included. That killed the parse before the includes ran, the search
    statement then failed on unregistered names, and the whole probe printed
    nothing at exit zero. Headers here get prose.

    RUN:   <binary> incant/litFlagProbe
*****************************************************************************/
register(Ladder);
define
    LitScaf isRule "x"-;
    ;
search reset stack Grokking Ladder UnitTests Utilities list;

print "== isLiteraL at the read sites ==":;
print "  Braced[1]   labelled literal   isLiteraL =" Braced[1].isLiteraL:;
print "  Braced[3]   labelled literal   isLiteraL =" Braced[3].isLiteraL:;
print "  Braced[2]   reference          isLiteraL =" Braced[2].isLiteraL:;
print "  LitScaf[1]  bare literal       isLiteraL =" LitScaf[1].isLiteraL:;
print "  Braced      the rule itself    isLiteraL =" Braced.isLiteraL:;
print "litFlagProbe SENTINEL":;
stop();
